fs require explicit justification in terms of net utility gains or losses. =
The absence of such analysis constitutes a partial divergence from the fram=
ework=E2=80=99s maximization imperative.</p><p>**Inclusivity and Moral Cons=
ideration**</p><p>The use case=E2=80=99s application of Karma to a large, h=
eterogeneous urban population aligns with classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99=
s inclusivity principle, which demands equal moral consideration for all af=
fected individuals. The mechanism=E2=80=99s design to allocate resources ba=
sed on urgency rather than socio-economic status reflects an inclusive appr=
oach to welfare.</p><p>However, the use case does not explicitly address th=
e moral status of non-human animals or other sentient beings potentially af=
fected by urban congestion and pollution. Classical utilitarianism extends =
moral consideration to all sentient beings capable of pleasure and pain. Wh=
ile this omission may be understandable given the use case=E2=80=99s focus,=
 it represents an under-specification relative to the framework=E2=80=99s i=
nclusivity demands.</p><p>**Transparency, Measurement, and Practical Decisi=
on Procedure**</p><p>Classical utilitarianism requires a decision procedure=
 that, in principle, can evaluate the utility consequences of actions. The =
use case provides a sophisticated software framework for modeling Karma eco=
