ime, financial costs, and urgency proxies.</p><p>The use case=E2=80=99s omi=
ssion of broader utility dimensions, such as psychological well-being, envi=
ronmental justice, and long-term societal impacts, limits its fidelity to c=
lassical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s comprehensive consequentialism. This unde=
r-specification may impair the system=E2=80=99s ability to fully maximize u=
tility.</p><p>**Moral Motivation and Compliance**</p><p>Classical utilitari=
anism faces challenges regarding moral motivation=E2=80=94why agents should=
 act to maximize collective utility. The use case models agents as rational=
 and self-interested, with incentives aligned through Karma balances and au=
ction mechanisms. This design reflects a sophisticated attempt to align ind=
ividual incentives with collective welfare, addressing a key utilitarian co=
ncern.</p><p>However, the use case does not explicitly address potential is=
sues of moral motivation beyond economic incentives, such as intrinsic mora=
l commitments or social norms. While utilitarianism often relies on externa=
l incentives, the framework acknowledges the complexity of moral motivation=
, which may be under-addressed here.</p><p>**Summary**</p><p>In sum, the Ka=
rma mechanism aligns strongly with classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s conse=
quentialist, impartial, and inclusivity principles by focusing on maximizin=
g social welfare through equitable resource allocation. Its innovative use =
of a non-monetary currency addresses equity concerns that monetary mechanis=
ms exacerbate, thus promoting fairness consistent with utilitarian impartia=
