atedly consume the resource, depleting Karma and limiting their future acce=
ss, while the low-urgency majority accumulates Karma by abstaining. However=
, if the high-urgency individuals=E2=80=99 needs are critical and time-sens=
itive, the system=E2=80=99s enforced rationing through Karma balances may d=
elay or restrict their access.</p><p>From a classical utilitarian perspecti=
ve, this could lead to suboptimal utility outcomes if urgent needs are unme=
t or delayed. The mechanism=E2=80=99s fairness-oriented constraints might i=
nhibit maximization of aggregate welfare by imposing equalizing restriction=
s that conflict with the imperative to prioritize the greatest utility gain=
s.</p><p>This edge case reveals a tension between the framework=E2=80=99s m=
aximization principle and the use case=E2=80=99s fairness constraints. It s=
uggests that without explicit prioritization mechanisms or exceptions for c=
ritical urgency, Karma may fail to maximize overall utility, contravening c=
lassical utilitarianism.</p><p>**Edge Case 2: Behavioral Non-Compliance and=
 Market Liquidity Collapse**</p><p>Consider a scenario where a significant =
portion of the population becomes inactive or disengaged from the Karma eco=
