ity.</p><p>2. **Assumption of Virtuous Behavior**: The proposal assumes tha=
t individuals will act virtuously by choosing not to consume resources when=
 possible. However, this assumption may not hold true in all cases, particu=
larly in high-pressure situations where individuals may prioritize their im=
mediate needs over collective well-being. The framework requires a more rob=
ust mechanism to ensure that individuals are motivated to act in accordance=
 with the virtues promoted by the Karma system.</p><p>3. **Potential for Re=
lativism**: The Karma economy's emphasis on fairness and equity may be inte=
rpreted differently across cultures and contexts. This raises questions abo=
ut the universality of the virtues being promoted. If different communities=
 prioritize different virtues, the Karma system may struggle to achieve con=
sistent ethical outcomes.</p><p>4. **Conflict of Virtues**: The framework d=
oes not adequately address the potential conflicts between different virtue=
s that may arise in the context of urban mobility. For example, the virtue =
of justice may conflict with the virtue of compassion when determining how =
to allocate resources among users with varying needs. The use case must pro=
vide a more nuanced understanding of how to navigate such conflicts.</p><h2=
>4) Stress Tests / Edge Cases</h2><p>To further evaluate the Karma economy =
under the virtue ethics framework, we can conceive of several scenarios tha=
t test its robustness.</p><h3>Scenario 1: High-Demand Situations</h3><p>Ima=
gine a scenario where a natural disaster occurs, leading to a sudden influx=
